    <briefdescription>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>This class constructs either a dominator or a post-dominator tree for a given function.</para><para>First, let us define &quot;Dominance.&quot; <ref refid="classA" kindref="compound">A</ref> node <ref refid="classX" kindref="compound">X</ref> in a <ref refid="classCFG" kindref="compound">CFG</ref> dominates node <ref refid="namespaceY" kindref="compound">Y</ref> if all paths into <ref refid="namespaceY" kindref="compound">Y</ref> must first pass through <ref refid="classX" kindref="compound">X</ref>. (Similarly, a node <ref refid="classX" kindref="compound">X</ref> post-dominates node <ref refid="namespaceY" kindref="compound">Y</ref> if all paths out of <ref refid="namespaceY" kindref="compound">Y</ref> must pass through <ref refid="classX" kindref="compound">X</ref>)</para><para>To understand a dominator tree, first the concept of immediate dominance must be understood: For a given node <ref refid="namespaceY" kindref="compound">Y</ref> in a <ref refid="classCFG" kindref="compound">CFG</ref>, its &quot;Immediate Dominator&quot; <ref refid="classX" kindref="compound">X</ref> is the nearest node which dominates <ref refid="namespaceY" kindref="compound">Y</ref>. (For example, within straight-line code, the Immediate Dominator of any statement is the statement immediately preceding; in an if-statement, the immediate dominator of the first statement in the true-body is the conditional). The dominator tree is thus a graph where the successors for a given node <ref refid="classX" kindref="compound">X</ref> are all the nodes for which <ref refid="classX" kindref="compound">X</ref> is the immediate dominator.</para><para><ref refid="classA" kindref="compound">A</ref> post-dominator tree is similar except calculated using post-dominators (hence, an immediate post-dominator for node <ref refid="classX" kindref="compound">X</ref> is the first statement <ref refid="namespaceY" kindref="compound">Y</ref> that must be executed after <ref refid="classX" kindref="compound">X</ref>). </para>    </detaileddescription>
